You should consider edge cases such as empty arrays and arrays with a single element.

### Solution:
```rust
pub fn search_in_rotated_array(arr: &[isize], targ: isize) -> Option<usize> {
    if arr.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }

    let (mut left, mut right) = (0, arr.len() - 1);

    while left <= right {
        let mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if arr[mid] == targ {
            return Some(mid);
        }

        if arr[left] == arr[mid] && arr[mid] == arr[right] {
            left += 1;
            right -= 1;
        } else if arr[left] <= arr[mid] {
            if arr[left] <= targ && targ < arr[mid] {
                right = mid - 1;
            } else {
                left = mid + 1;
            }
        } else {
            if arr[mid] < targ && targ <= arr[right] {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }

    None
}
```

This solution efficiently handles the rotated sorted array with duplicate elements using a modified binary search algorithm. It considers the edge cases of empty arrays and arrays with a single element, and returns the index of the target element if found, or `None` if the target element is not present.

