### Solution:
To find the second largest element in a binary search tree, we can perform a reverse in-order traversal of the tree. In a reverse in-order traversal, we visit the right subtree, then the current node, and finally the left subtree.

Here's the implementation of the `findSecondLargest` method using reverse in-order traversal:

```javascript
findSecondLargest() {
    if (!this.root || (!this.root.left && !this.root.right)) {
        throw new Error('Tree must have at least 2 nodes');
    }

    let secondLargest = null;
    let count = 0;

    const reverseInOrderTraversal = (node) => {
        if (node && count < 2) {
            reverseInOrderTraversal(node.right);
            count++;
            if (count === 2) {
                secondLargest = node.value;
                return;
            }
            reverseInOrderTraversal(node.left);
        }
    };

    reverseInOrderTraversal(this.root);

    if (count < 2) {
        throw new Error('Tree must have at least 2 distinct nodes');
    }

    return secondLargest;
}
```

In this solution, we use a recursive reverse in-order traversal to find the second largest element in the binary search tree. If the tree has less than 2 nodes or less than 2 distinct nodes, an error is thrown. Otherwise, the second largest element is returned.

