### Solution:
```java
public void calculateWheelSpeeds(double desiredLinearVelocity, double desiredAngularVelocity) {
    // Calculate the feedforward component
    double feedforwardLeft = m_feedforward.calculate(desiredLinearVelocity, desiredAngularVelocity);
    double feedforwardRight = m_feedforward.calculate(desiredLinearVelocity, desiredAngularVelocity);

    // Calculate the feedback component using PID controller
    double feedbackLeft = m_rightMasterPIDController.calculate(m_odometry.getPoseMeters().getRotation().getRadians(), desiredAngularVelocity);
    double feedbackRight = m_rightMasterPIDController.calculate(m_odometry.getPoseMeters().getRotation().getRadians(), desiredAngularVelocity);

    // Calculate the differential drive kinematics
    ChassisSpeeds chassisSpeeds = new ChassisSpeeds(desiredLinearVelocity, 0, desiredAngularVelocity);
    DifferentialDriveWheelSpeeds wheelSpeeds = m_kinematics.toWheelSpeeds(chassisSpeeds);

    // Combine feedforward and feedback components to get final wheel speeds
    double leftWheelSpeed = wheelSpeeds.leftMetersPerSecond + feedforwardLeft + feedbackLeft;
    double rightWheelSpeed = wheelSpeeds.rightMetersPerSecond + feedforwardRight + feedbackRight;

    // Set the calculated wheel speeds to the motors
    setLeftWheelSpeed(leftWheelSpeed);
    setRightWheelSpeed(rightWheelSpeed);
}
```

In the solution, the method `calculateWheelSpeeds` first calculates the feedforward component using the provided feedforward constants and desired velocities. Then, it calculates the feedback component using the PID controller based on the current orientation and desired angular velocity. Next, it uses the differential drive kinematics to convert the desired linear and angular velocities into left and right wheel speeds. Finally, it combines the feedforward and feedback components to obtain the final wheel speeds and sets them to the motors.

